  "result": "On the last day of the laundromat, somebody brought in a chair.\n\nIt was a kitchen chair, yellow, with one leg shorter than the others. The man who carried it in set it beside Dryer 6 and sat down.\n\n\u201cYou know we\u2019re closing,\u201d said Bea, who owned the place.\n\n\u201cI know,\u201d he said. \u201cI\u2019ve got forty minutes.\u201d\n\nHe had brought no laundry.\n\nBy noon there were four chairs. A woman from the bakery brought a folding stool and a paper bag of rolls she couldn\u2019t sell because they had stuck together in the oven. Bea pulled them apart. The places where they\u2019d joined were soft and white.\n\nNobody made a speech. People kept getting up to check clothes that weren\u2019t there.\n\nAt three, a boy asked Bea what she was going to do with the clock. It had been seven minutes fast for as long as anyone could remember.\n\n\u201cYou can have it,\u201d she said.\n\nHe took it down and turned the little wheel on the back.\n\n\u201cDon\u2019t fix it,\u201d said the man in the yellow chair.\n\nThe boy looked at Bea.\n\n\u201cHe\u2019s right,\u201d she said. \u201cYou\u2019ll miss the bus.\u201d",
